AB  - Navigating an aircraft on the airport surface is a cognitively demanding task, particularly at large or unfamiliar airfields where complex layouts and dense traffic increase the burden on flight crews. Existing pilot-aid approaches provide only limited support and still require crews to manually translate clearances into taxi routes, increasing workload and risk of misunderstanding. This paper introduces Speech-to-Route, the first pipeline that infers layout-consistent taxi routes from taxi instructions. The method comprises three modules. It develops an ASR module that combines a semi-supervised acoustic model with a domain-adapted language model to mitigate domain mismatch and the scarcity of labeled data. The multi-task NLU module is designed to extract taxi clearances by jointly predicting speaker role, intent, and named entities from controller–pilot communication. A graph-constrained route inference module with an error-aware refinement mechanism is proposed to convert these extracted clearances into layout-consistent taxi routes. The method demonstrates strong performance across all modules. The ASR module achieves word error rates of 5.12% on a public ATC dataset and 6.21% on a privately collected Changi Airport dataset. The downstream modules remain reliable despite noise propagated from upstream modules. The extracted taxi clearances reach an F1-score of 99.04%, and the final inferred routes achieve accuracy of 99.08% with the intended ground-truth routes, corresponding to 108 correctly reconstructed movements out of 109. The proposed method has the potential to enhance safety and efficiency by minimizing communication errors, and improving situational awareness.
