AB  - Vehicle Re-Identification (Re-ID) in unconstrained environments remains challenging because mass-produced vehicles often exhibit highly similar global appearances while differing in sparse, localised details. Although modern visual backbones provide strong retrieval representations, conventional benchmarks offer limited semantic supervision for modelling such fine-grained cues. To complement rather than replace visual evidence, we propose the Linguistically Driven Visual Query Network (LD-VQN), a multimodal framework that integrates hierarchical visual representations with optional linguistic guidance. First, we introduce three novel T2I-VeRi-776, T2I-VehicleID, and T2I-VeRi-Wild datasets, derived from VeRi-776, VehicleID, and VeRi-Wild, respectively, and including free-form textual extensions comprising more than 808,000 natural language descriptions. LD-VQN employs a Multi-Level Feature Adapter (MLA) to recover hierarchical visual cues and a Dynamic Text-Conditioned Routing (D-TCR) module to modulate latent component interactions according to query semantics. A Gram-based orthogonality regulariser further reduces directional redundancy among visually grounded component nodes. The results indicate that language acts as a complementary semantic signal whose benefits increase under more challenging retrieval conditions. All headline results are obtained under a symmetric multimodal protocol in which both query and gallery samples carry a caption. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that incorporating linguistic guidance yields consistent improvements, with LD-VQN achieving mAP scores of 80.73%, 89.64%, and 58.91% on T2I-VeRi-776, T2I-VehicleID, and T2I-VeRi-Wild, respectively.
