AB  - This study proposes a multi-objective training framework that internalizes persona knowledge — an individual’s beliefs, judgment criteria, and speech patterns — directly into the parameters of small language models (sLLMs), eliminating the need for lengthy context injection at inference time. A 12B sLLM trained with the proposed method and performing zero-context inference outperformed GPT-4o and Claude 3.5 Sonnet under random-sampled context, and remained competitive against the stronger BM25-retrieved variants of these SOTA APIs at the same 1500-token budget—outperforming both on utterance verification (QB-YN, QB-YN-v2) with statistical significance against GPT-4o+BM25 at the speaker level, exceeding GPT-4o+BM25 numerically on value judgment (LLM-Gen), while being slightly surpassed by Claude+BM25 on this task. The 12B model also surpassed a 27B base model with random-sampled in-context learning by +0.193 on value judgment under a fixed mean-over-epochs evaluation protocol. Most existing approaches to individual simulation rely on injecting past utterances into the prompt, an approach that increases token costs and readily undermines persona consistency when contextual cues conflict. To address these limitations, we combine classification, contrastive learning, and language modeling losses to simultaneously optimize correctness, discriminability, and fluency, and propose an evaluation protocol that systematically varies context availability and name dependence. Experiments on 15 public figures drawn from QuoteBank-family corpora demonstrate that internalization training simultaneously improves both efficiency and robustness over context-based imitation, while name dependence analysis reveals that a substantial portion of SOTA models’ zero-shot accuracy stems from pretraining memorization rather than from utterance-level reasoning about the persona.
