UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2451958826002563
KW  - AI ethics
KW  - Ethical principles
KW  - Ethical prioritization
KW  - Ethical consistency
KW  - LLM ethical behavior
AB  - As Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems become increasingly autonomous, their decisions are expected to comply with ethical frameworks grounded in widely accepted principles such as beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, autonomy, and explicability. While these principles are commonly referenced in regulation and provider policies, the prioritization among them is rarely made explicit. This paper examines how different prioritizations of ethical principles within the same ethical framework affect the behavior, consistency, and governance of Large Language Models (LLMs), and identifies regulatory, policy, and implementation gaps related to ethical prioritization. We follow a three-phase methodology. First, we analyze AI ethics regulation and standards, to assess how ethical principles and their prioritization are treated. Second, we examine the clarity, completeness and transparency of published policies of major LLM providers regarding AI ethics. To achieve this, we combine manual analysis of published documents with direct queries to the models regarding their ethical frameworks, priorities, and verification mechanisms. Third, we empirically evaluate eight popular LLMs using five domain-specific ethical dilemmas, to assess their behavior under different ethical principle prioritizations, within the same ethical framings. By systematically modifying the prioritization of ethical principles, we assess the effects on the decision outcomes, consistency and convergence of responses, within and across models and user runs. Our findings reveal that although existing regulations and operational policies define ethical principles, they rarely define ethical prioritization structures, either generic or context-specific. Empirically, altering the prioritization of ethical principles within the same ethical model, leads to decision reversals, reduced internal consistency, and increased divergence both within and across LLMs. These effects persist even among models claiming dynamic ethical reasoning. The results highlight ethical prioritization as a critical yet under-specified factor in AI governance and underscore the need for: (i) formalized and sector-specific regulation; (ii) well-defined and transparent corporate policies; and (iii) training and implementation of continuously verifiable, context-aware ethical models for critical applications.
