AB  - The acceleration of molecular discovery and chemical systems engineering is increasingly dependent on the availability of high-fidelity, structured relational datasets. Despite the wealth of information contained within the scientific literature, the extraction of complex chemical-property relationships remains a significant bottleneck owing to linguistic nuance, domain-specific nomenclature, and the structural heterogeneity of technical documents. Conventional supervised pipelines demand vast annotated corpora, while general-purpose foundation models frequently overgeneralize and hallucinate when applied directly to such specialized tasks. Building on a previously released single-pass fine-tuned extractor for fuel ignition properties (IgnitionGPT), this work introduces an autonomous agentic framework based on a Generative-Reflective architecture that wraps two cooperative agents(both instantiated from a fine-tuned GPT-4.1 mini) around a centralized relational store: (i) a Generator Agent that produces an initial structured relation classification, and (ii) a Refection Agent that iteratively critiques and refines the output against the source text. The framework is evaluated on the same human-annotated corpus of 304 heterogeneous sources (263 peer-reviewed articles, 41 patents) covering 581 compounds and three concurrent target properties (Research Octane Number, Motor Octane Number, and Cetane Number). Using token-level precision, recall, and F1 against a document-disjoint held-out split, we compare three settings: (a) zero-shot GPT-4.1 mini, (b) single-pass supervised fine-tuning, and (c) the proposed Generator-Reflector loop. The zero-shot baseline reaches only 47.8% F1, consistent with overgeneralized token boundaries. Single-pass fine-tuning saturates at the empirical performance ceiling of the test set using approximately 30 training documents (≈10% of the corpus). The proposed agentic loop reaches the same saturation using 24 documents (≈8%), corresponding to roughly a 20% relative reduction in the minimum supervision required, and reduces relation-misattribution errors observed in single-pass outputs. We discuss the architectural origin of these gains and the limitations associated with small validation sets, proprietary fine-tuning, and the modest absolute size of the improvement.
