AB  - The emergence of website text-mining technologies for identifying innovative companies promised to mitigate the shortcomings of current approaches based on structured surveys, patents, and financial data. However, the innovation mining community increasingly raises concerns about the validity of current approaches, arguing that models rely on accidental keywords rather than substantive innovation cues when predicting firm innovation labels. This paper proposes a theory-informed alternative for innovation mining by introducing the strategic signaling-based Website Innovation Footprint concept to guide empirical efforts. The study deployed a sample of 2070 innovative and non-innovative companies’ websites and revealed that a fine-tuned evidence-producing LLM was able to classify them with 90% accuracy. Further, the results show that while TF–IDF in combination with Random Forest achieved 85% accuracy on the test dataset, the explainability of the predictors was weakly related to substantive innovation cues. This study contributes to the innovation mining approach by proposing a concept of Website Innovation Footprint and an alternative method for identifying innovative companies.
