AB  - The rapid growth of exposed application programming interfaces (APIs) has made ingress-side risk detection a critical function for network service management. However, existing detection methods face a fundamental deployment dilemma: lightweight models are efficient but often lack semantic discriminability, whereas deep or Transformer-based models provide stronger representation capacity at prohibitive online inference cost. This paper introduces SwiftAPI, a confidence-guided hybrid framework for scalable API risk detection from request URLs. A lightweight logistic regression classifier rapidly handles high-confidence requests using character-level n-gram term frequency–inverse document frequency (TF-IDF) representations, while only uncertain cases are routed to a Qwen3-0.6B small language model (SLM) tuned through low-rank adaptation (LoRA) for semantic re-evaluation. By allocating computationally intensive semantic reasoning according to decision uncertainty, this design avoids invoking the SLM for most requests. Experiments on eight public datasets show that SwiftAPI improves the average F1-score over the strongest external baseline while substantially reducing inference latency. Routing analysis further reveals that the SLM processes only 7.00% of samples on average while yielding a net gain in correct predictions on every dataset. Real-traffic analysis estimates a sustainable single-instance throughput of approximately 6.7 Gb/s, suggesting that uncertainty-based allocation of semantic inference can reduce computational overhead and provide a practical path toward scalable API risk detection at high-throughput network service entry points.
