DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipm.2026.104819
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306457326002104
KW  - Natural language processing
KW  - Large language models
KW  - Machine translation
KW  - Agent
AB  - Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated unprecedented capabilities in machine translation. However, their practical deployment in high-stakes applications is critically hindered by the generation of “hallucinations”—content detached from the source text, which severely compromises translational faithfulness. Furthermore, relying on unconditional self-correction often proves counterproductive, as models may blindly alter originally faithful translations or overlook subtle errors without explicit multi-dimensional guidance. To address this fundamental limitation, we propose AMART, a Multi-Agent Reflective framework designed to instill a structured, two-stage deliberative process into the translation workflow of LLMs. In AMART, we first employ a multi-agent detection system where specialized agents, operating at complementary granularities (sentence, chunk, and word), collaboratively scrutinize an initial translation for potential faithfulness deviations. If an error is flagged, AMART initiates a conditional reflective process, which first prompts the LLM to analyze and articulate the nature of the identified error, and only then guides it to regenerate a corrected translation based on its own reflection. This mechanism compels the model to form a metacognitive understanding of its failure modes, moving beyond simple error correction. Extensive experiments on challenging hallucination benchmarks show that AMART improves hallucination detection accuracy by up to 1.3-fold and substantially reduces faithfulness errors. On the WMT24++ and FLORES-200 test sets, the framework consistently improves overall translation quality and achieves state-of-the-art performance, outperforming strong baselines by up to 2.2 BLEU and 2.4 COMET points. Ablation and efficiency analyses further indicate that the conditionally triggered architecture allows diagnostic signals to guide targeted corrections, improving semantic faithfulness while keeping inference cost manageable.
ER  - 

