AB  - Substation safety inspection is essential for the stable operation of power systems. Conventional inspection models rely on annotated data but often generalize poorly across equipment types, anomaly patterns, and inspection tasks. Multimodal large language models provide broader image-and-language reasoning ability, yet their industrial inspection performance remains limited without structured domain grounding. To address this gap, we propose iSAFE (Intelligent Substation Anomaly-aware Framework with Knowledge Enhancement), a structured knowledge augmentation framework for substation safety inspection. It converts legacy detection annotations into a two-layer knowledge base of factual visual cases and conceptual anomaly knowledge. At inference time, it retrieves relevant evidence and uses an evidence-guided pipeline to support a target multimodal large language model without fine-tuning. We construct SafeSubstation-Bench, a benchmark with 8307 images and 36,238 question–answer pairs across six inspection tasks. A separate proprietary multimodal model is used only offline to generate candidate question–answer pairs, which are then rule-filtered and manually reviewed. Experiments show that the proposed framework improves substation inspection accuracy over direct baselines, supporting retrieval-based knowledge augmentation for industrial artificial intelligence in safety-critical inspection.
