AB  - Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have led to rapid adoption of abstractive news summarisation, yet they also amplify misinformation because models may hallucinate facts or copy unverified statements. Current summarisation systems treat every input document as fully trustworthy, even though modern news streams contain misleading or fake stories; this increases the risk of unfaithful summaries. We frame news summarisation as a causal chain where unverified facts trigger downstream hallucinations. To break this chain, we propose a causality-aware framework that detects and removes fake news before summarisation. A lightweight pipeline performs named-entity recognition, relation and causal fact extraction, then learns causal graphs to distinguish genuine and fake assertions; the pipeline relies on agentic small models for entity recognition and information extraction. Verified causal facts are passed to an LLM summariser that produces an abstractive summary conditioned on trusted information. Extensive experiments conducted on six publicly available cross-lingual news summarization datasets demonstrate the robustness of our framework. Evaluations across seven complementary metrics reveal that CaHa-Summ consistently outperforms LLM-only baselines, achieving 4-6-point gains on ROUGE and BERTScore and notable improvements in factual consistency as reflected by FactCC and BARTScore. Ablation studies reveal that NER-driven causal extraction is critical for cross-domain fake news detection, and human evaluation confirms that readers perceive our summaries as more trustworthy. Our work highlights the importance of causal reasoning and agentic information extraction in summarisation pipelines and offers a promising path for safe and faithful news delivery.
ER  - 

