AB  - Video Question Answering (VideoQA) demands precise grounding of question-relevant evidence, adaptive modeling of temporally evolving scenes, and rigorous alignment between visual semantics and query intent. Existing approaches remain constrained by three inherent limitations: (i) imprecise question-guided grounding, where only a few frames contain crucial evidence yet existing models process full videos indiscriminately, leading to visual noise and missed decisive moments; (ii) rigid temporal modeling, since fixed or uniform event segmentation fails to adapt to dynamic scene transitions or question-specific temporal demands; and (iii) misaligned textual granularity, as most systems generate descriptions with a one-size-fits-all strategy, ignoring that fine-grained and coarse-grained questions require different levels of detail. To address these challenges, we propose HAViG, a Hierarchical Adaptive Visual Grounding framework that unifies precise keyframe selection, adaptive temporal contextualization, and question-aware description filtering. A hierarchical keyframe router identifies and re-evaluates question-critical frames with high fidelity, while a question-guided contextualizer selectively enriches or reconstructs temporal context based on frame-question relevance. Additionally, a tailored question-guided description filtering mechanism (Que-Filtering) refines LLM-generated descriptions to align semantic detail with the query intent. Extensive evaluation on two VideoQA benchmarks confirms that HAViG consistently surpasses 22 competitive baselines, delivering efficient and semantically robust reasoning without model fine-tuning or large-scale LLM dependency. The source code is available at https://github.com/LingminPan/HVAA.
