AB  - Carbon emission monitoring of urban construction lacks dynamism and regional adaptability. This limits its effectiveness in supporting policy implementation during low-carbon urban transitions. Existing methods predominantly rely on static inventory approaches, overlooking construction boundaries within policy contexts and failing to adequately address local variations. Consequently, governance needs across construction lifecycle remain insufficiently met. This study proposes a semantics-driven, dynamic identification method for carbon emission monitoring indicators to enhance policy coordination, responsiveness, and regional suitability. Based on a goal-oriented and responsibility-clarifying management framework, a four-layer structure covering content, responsibility, indicators, and regional differentiation is developed and applied to analyze 198 urban low-carbon construction policy documents. Leveraging large language models, 46 groups of semantic terms representing construction content and development pathways are extracted. A policy filtering model based on content and hierarchical attributes is established, enabling multi-level, indicator-guided policy selection. Quantitative analysis objectively quantifies management responsibilities, revealing that the Ministry of Housing and Urban-Rural Development holds primary responsibility for 93 % of construction content, while 98 % involves co-management across multiple departments. Integrating this model with the scope of construction activities, a comprehensive set of 70 monitoring indicators covering construction process is formulated. The proposed method was applied in case studies in Huzhou and Bozhou. Results demonstrate its effectiveness in capturing inter-city differences, dynamic evolution of construction activities, and emerging development scenarios. Compared to existing approaches, this method improves indicator coverage by 167 % and granularity by 350 %, providing robust support for evaluation and management of construction activities across diverse cities and development stages.
