AB  - Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) swarms have evolved from single-platform operations to heterogeneous collaborative systems, significantly enhancing mission capabilities in complex environments. This survey provides a comprehensive review of task allocation methods for heterogeneous UAV swarms, addressing static, dynamic, and cross-domain scenarios involving diverse unmanned platforms such as ground and surface vehicles. We establish a standardized modeling framework that formally defines UAV attributes, task specifications, environmental constraints, and typical objective functions. The surveyed algorithms are taxonomically classified into six categories, i.e., classical optimization, intelligent optimization, market-based, clustering-based, learning-based, and game-theory-based methods, and are critically evaluated across multiple dimensions such as optimality, scalability, and computational efficiency. Beyond qualitative assessment, we extract and aggregate quantitative performance metrics from the literature to offer a benchmark-oriented analysis that guides practical algorithm selection. We further identify key future research directions, including ultra-large-scale swarm scalability, robust decision-making under uncertainty, field validation through hardware-in-the-loop and real-world testing, as well as the integration of emerging technologies such as large language models, reconfigurable intelligent surfaces, and semantic communication. By synthesizing the state of the art and outlining open challenges, this survey serves as a foundational reference for researchers and practitioners in autonomous swarm systems.
