AB  - We propose a Quantum-Enhanced Framework for Sentiment-Informed Market Regime Forecasting that integrates Transformer-based financial sentiment modeling, probabilistic regime detection, quantum-enhanced optimization, and deep learning into a unified forecasting pipeline. Financial sentiment is first extracted by evaluating transformer-based financial language models, with the highest-performing model selected to generate sentiment scores from financial text. These sentiment signals are then aligned with the American Association of Individual Investors (AAII) Investor Sentiment Survey to construct a consistent investor sentiment dataset spanning historical market conditions. The resulting sentiment features, together with historical S&P 500 price data, are processed using a Gaussian Hidden Markov Model (HMM) to identify latent market regimes. For each detected regime, a classical Monte Carlo simulation generates diverse price trajectories whose statistics are modulated by regime-specific sentiment. The framework is evaluated on 2,028 weekly S&P 500 observations spanning July 1987 to June 2026 using expanding-window walk-forward validation, producing 806 out-of-sample forecasts across five folds, with Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU), and Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) architectures each assessed under four component configurations. Regime-conditioned Monte Carlo augmentation lowers pooled root mean squared error (RMSE) in five of six paired comparisons, by between 5.3% and 38.9%, and Diebold–Mariano tests confirm that every configuration significantly outperformed by the reference model omits this component. The Quantum-Enhanced Adam optimizer proves architecture-dependent by contrast: it improves GRU and BiLSTM but degrades LSTM, and its effect on LSTM accuracy is not statistically significant. The strongest configuration overall, BiLSTM with Monte Carlo augmentation and Quantum-Enhanced Adam, attains an RMSE of 164.25, a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) of 4.43%, and an R2 of 0.9894. The findings indicate that coupling behavioral sentiment validation with regime-aware uncertainty modeling can improve practical market-decision support, and that scenario-based feature augmentation transfers more reliably across sequence architectures than optimizer-level modification does. We further observe three- to five-fold variation in fold-level RMSE for fixed configurations, indicating that single-split evaluations of financial forecasting models may substantially misstate expected performance. The study is limited to a single market index and uses simulated quantum circuits rather than current quantum hardware. Future work should test the framework across additional markets, characterize the conditions under which quantum-enhanced optimization benefits particular architectures, and evaluate the optimizer on emerging quantum hardware.
