AB  - Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (MM-RAG) grounds vision-language models in external evidence, yet mainstream linear frameworks may struggle to adapt retrieval and fusion to the varying visual and textual requirements of scientific questions. Specifically, current systems may suffer from blind fusion due to static modality weights, cross-modal misalignment when rerankers fail to detect inconsistent evidence, and open-loop retrieval errors that propagate into generation. To address these limitations, Reweight, Rerank, and Reflect-Augmented Generation (R3-RAG) is proposed as a closed-loop multimodal RAG framework that coordinates three modules for evidence-calibrated scientific reasoning. To support evidence-calibrated scientific reasoning, it integrates intent-aware dynamic fusion to adaptively align modal focus, coupled with an uncertainty-aware cascade reranker to filter potentially inconsistent cross-modal evidence. Furthermore, the system employs a diagnose-rewrite-route reflection loop to support iterative correction of retrieval failures. Experiments show that R3-RAG achieves a competitive 91.51% accuracy on the ScienceQA benchmark and outperforms existing baselines on the MMMU test set official Science discipline subset, reaching 48.21% accuracy. The results suggest that the framework improves reliability by reducing non-dominant modality noise and by revisiting low-confidence retrieval results before final answer generation.
