AB  - This research develops an intelligent decision-support system to address the critical challenges in port state control inspections for liquefied natural gas carriers. By integrating a fine-tuned Vision-Language Model with a multimodal knowledge graph through a novel collaborative workflow, the system overcomes the limitations of traditional experience-dependent inspection methods. It introduces three key innovations: a high-quality, expert-curated dataset for domain adaptation; a sequential optimization strategy combining supervised learning and reinforcement learning to align model outputs with inspector preferences; and a reliability-driven architecture that maintains parallel operation between knowledge retrieval and decision-making components. Experimental validation shows significant performance improvements, with SFT enhancing ROUGE-L and BERTScore metrics by 17.9% and 3.9%, respectively, and direct preference optimization further boosting accuracy, practicality, and consistency by 12.2%, 23.7%, and 23.1%. A comprehensive evaluation regimen, incorporating both automated metrics and expert-based assessment, demonstrated the system's superior overall effectiveness and reliability across all key performance dimensions. It provides theoretical reference for maritime safety AI and a practical tool for frontline PSC officers. This system transforms PSC inspections from reactive, subjective activities into proactive, knowledge-backed processes, providing precise technical terminology, verifiable regulatory citations, and risk-aware prioritization that outperforms general-purpose models in content precision and regulatory traceability. The research creates a robust framework for AI-assisted maritime safety oversight, bridging innovation and practical use while ensuring reliability throughout its lifecycle.
