AB  - Digital inpainting offers an efficient and non-invasive solution for mural restoration. Despite recent progress, existing mural inpainting methods still suffer from semantic inconsistency, style discontinuity, and blurred texture reconstruction. To address these challenges, we propose an adaptive multimodal diffusion framework that synergistically integrates high-level semantic knowledge with structural priors to guide the restoration process. Specifically, we design an inpainting-oriented structured prompt strategy that exploits the strong reasoning capability of a multimodal large language model (MLLM) to generate fine-grained high-level semantic descriptions. Concurrently, a masked autoencoder is utilized to reconstruct the global structural layout, providing the spatial geometry that textual descriptions lack. We introduce a Bidirectional Cross-modal Interaction Module (BCIM) that performs bidirectional cross-attention between textual and visual features to produce complementary multimodal representations. To effectively inject multimodal priors into the diffusion model, we further design an Adaptive Multimodal Prior Selection and Injection Module (AMPSIM) that selects informative cues while suppressing low-frequency interference. We further construct CNMural-MM, a diverse multimodal mural dataset tailored for inpainting. Extensive experiments on CNMural-MM and several public benchmarks demonstrate that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance and exhibits strong generalization ability.
