SN  - 0925-2312
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2026.133234
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925231226006314
KW  - Malware detection
KW  - API call sequence
KW  - Large language model
KW  - Prompt engineering
KW  - Embedding learning
AB  - Increasing API call sequence-based malware detection methods attempt to incorporate fine-grained semantic information, i.e., parameters and return values, but face two core challenges: semantic complexity and state explosion, which stem from the difficulty in modeling precise security-aware semantics for domain-specific parameters and return values and the exponential growth of semantic state space. To address these issues, we propose a novel paradigm, SaBER-LLMs, which endows large language models with three vital roles: security experts, sensitivity assessors, and dual-view semantic perceptors to leverage their strong semantic understanding and reasoning capabilities, fulfilled through three core components: (i) Dual-stage prompt engineering translates low-level behavior triplets into concise, human-readable, security-aware descriptions and assigns them interpretable risk levels. (ii) Two-stage compression strategy extracts high-risk behaviors to effectively mitigate state explosion. (iii) Dual-view combined embedding encodes both atomic intent and contextual semantics for accurate and resilient detection. Extensive experiments show the superiority of SaBER-LLMs in detection performance, interpretability, and robustness against three typical adversarial attacks, i.e., injection, substitution, and parameter-level perturbation, outperforming existing state-of-the-art methods. Specifically, SaBER-LLMs achieves 97.71% and 96.43% accuracy over two baselines, reduces downstream classifier inference time, and maintains high defense effectiveness under both black-box and grey-box threat models with impractically large attack overheads, underscoring the innovative paradigm in leveraging LLMs for heightened security efficacy and its practical advantage in real-world threat scenarios.
